#
# PROBLEM: the same screen text needs different escapes in the two
# condition spellings. Strings keep backslashes literal (a DOS
# kindness); regexes are raw Python re, where a backslash is an
# operator. The two regimes sit side by side in every DOS script.

description "Two escaping regimes, one screen"
platform    dos
timeout     30s

wait "C:\>"          # string rule: backslash LITERAL by default
wait /C:\\>/         # regex rule: backslash must be DOUBLED

# The same path, both ways:
wait "A:\GAMES\README.TXT"      # literal text — reads like DOS
wait /A:\\GAMES\\.*\.TXT/       # pattern — every \ doubles, except
                                # the \. which is a regex escape;
                                # three backslash meanings, one line

# Subtlety at the boundary: the slash delimiter's ONLY escape is
# \/ — every other backslash passes through to Python re. And #
# is pattern text inside the fences but a comment outside them:
wait /ERROR #[0-9]+/    # this # matches a literal #...
wait "ERROR"            # ...this one starts a comment ->  # like so

# The redesign made regexes instantly RECOGNIZABLE (/.../ vs the
# old regex-keyword string), which was the CLARITY goal — but it
# did not make them consistently WRITABLE next to strings: the
# author must context-switch escaping rules on adjacent lines,
# and DOS text is backslash-dense in exactly the wrong way.
#
# OPEN QUESTIONS: is a raw-regex form worth it? (Raw strings were
# deleted from the language as an unearned token class.) Should
# the spec instead bless a convention — prefer literal strings for
# anything containing a path, regexes only for genuinely variable
# text — and have validation hint when a regex contains \\ runs
# that a literal match would serve? Any auto-normalizing of
# backslashes inside /.../ was rejected: it would fork reliquary's
# regex dialect from Python's, a worse inconsistency than this one.
